---
import { Arrow } from "./ui/icons";

const faq = [
  {
    question: "What is Bitropy?",
    answer:
      "Bitropy is an enterprise LLM gateway that sits between your applications and every AI model provider. It handles authentication, routing, caching, policy enforcement, audit logging, and usage tracking — so that logic lives in one place instead of leaking into every service. An MCP gateway is included for teams that use Model Context Protocol tools.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I keep using the OpenAI SDK with Bitropy?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Bitropy exposes an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, so you change the base URL in your existing SDK (Python, TypeScript, Go, or raw HTTP) and everything continues to work. Aliases let you address models by role (e.g. 'team-default') instead of hard-coding provider model names.",
  },
  {
    question: "Which LLM providers does Bitropy support?",
    answer:
      "OpenAI, Anthropic, Google (Gemini), Mistral, Groq, xAI, Azure OpenAI, AWS Bedrock, OpenRouter, and Ollama out of the box. Any OpenAI-compatible backend can be added through a custom base URL.",
  },
  {
    question: "How does Bitropy handle authentication and policy?",
    answer:
      "Bitropy issues scoped API keys per team, app, or user and resolves them against central policies before any request reaches a provider. Policies include PII redaction, content filters, data residency, rate limits, retention, and per-key spend limits. Every request is logged with correlation IDs for audit.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I track usage per team, app, or user?",
    answer:
      "Yes. Bitropy captures tokens, cost, latency, and model per request and attributes it to the identity that made the call. Metrics are visible in the admin console and exportable to Prometheus, BigQuery, Snowflake, or your BI tool of choice.",
  },
  {
    question: "What does the MCP gateway add?",
    answer:
      "The MCP gateway routes Model Context Protocol traffic through the same auth, audit, and policy layer as LLM traffic. You get a central registry of MCP servers, isolation per tool, per-user approval flows, and a single audit trail for both model calls and tool invocations.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I deploy and operate Bitropy?",
    answer:
      "Bitropy runs as a self-hosted deployment in your VPC (Docker, Kubernetes, or a single binary) or as a managed service we operate. There is no required vendor database — state can live in your Postgres / Redis, and the gateway scales horizontally behind any load balancer.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is Bitropy suitable for regulated environments?",
    answer:
      "Yes. The self-hosted deployment keeps prompts, responses, and audit data inside your environment. Central policy enforcement maps cleanly to SOC 2, ISO 27001, ISO/IEC 42000, and EU AI Act controls, and the audit trail is designed to be the system of record for AI usage.",
  },
];
---

<section class="mt-24 md:mt-32">
  <div class="text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <h2 class="text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight">
      Bitropy FAQ
    </h2>
    <p class="text-lg mt-4 text-slate-600">
      Common questions about deployment, provider switching, policy, observability, MCP, and the roadmap.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid gap-3 max-w-3xl mx-auto mt-12">
    {
      faq.map((item) => (
        <details class="group border border-slate-200 rounded-xl bg-white">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold cursor-pointer list-none p-5 text-slate-800">
            <span>{item.question}</span>
            <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-slate-400">
              <Arrow class="w-5 h-5" />
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p class="text-slate-600 px-5 pb-5 leading-relaxed">
            {item.answer}
          </p>
        </details>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>
